    sim_modelo_teorico: "not validated against real floods",
    sim_limites: "<b>What it assumes and what it does not cover:</b> rain falls on the chosen circle and no water flows in from outside (a small radius does not receive runoff from its catchment); drainage is assumed uniform, 10 mm/h over impervious surfaces, regardless of river level or blocked drains; channels are carved with an assumed width of about 10 m; isolated people and health access are measured from the circle's edge; validation used 150 m cells, the simulator uses 34-60 m. It is an exploration tool, not an official forecast.", sim_modelo_ciudad: (c, v) => `validated in ${c} (CSI ${v})`,
    sim_modelo_otras: (n) => `validated in ${n} other cities, not in this one`,
    sim_validez_res: (n, casos, pod, far, csi, jrc, mejores, rho, mejor) => `<b>Validation:</b> El Niño 2015-16 and 2023-24 were replayed in ${n} cities with real rain and river levels and compared with the water seen by satellite (Dynamic World). In the ${casos} cases with enough observed water it detected ${pod} % of the wet cells, with ${far} % false alarms (CSI ${csi}); the «where water has been before» reference gives CSI ${jrc} and the model beats it in ${mejores} of ${casos}. Across cities, simulated wet area follows the observed one (correlation ${rho}). ${mejor ? "" : "<b>Bottom line: it's guidance; to know where water lingers for weeks, the «Previously flooded» layer is more reliable.</b>"}`,
    sim_modelo_sin_agua: (c) => `in ${c} satellites saw almost no water in those seasons: it couldn't be validated here`,
