    info_esc: "Each slider runs from the lowest to the highest value recorded here in El Niño seasons since 1997-98 (with no record, an assumed range, flagged below). Wind is not included: it does not change how much rain water pools; a storm surge is already the river or sea level.",
    info_b_mm: "Total storm rain. Source: IMERG satellite (NASA) on ~11 km cells, which smooths peaks lasting minutes; ERA5 reanalysis in 1997-98. The maximum is the most that fell in that many hours in any El Niño here.",
    info_b_horas: "Over how many hours the rain falls. With an El Niño chosen, the real duration of its worst storm (first to last hour with rain); moving it stretches or shrinks the same shape.",
    info_b_theta: "Soil moisture just before the storm (ERA5, 0-7 cm), as % of what the soil can hold. Wetter: less infiltration and more runoff.",
    info_b_lagos: "Pond level before the storm: full or up to 1.5 m below the bank. Their levels are not recorded: assumed range.",
    info_b_rio: "River level at the nearest INA gauge (within 15 km), measured or estimated with GloFAS on each storm's days. At its median level the water stays in the channel; at evacuation it reaches the low banks; above that, each extra metre adds up.",
    info_b_crecida: "No INA gauge nearby: metres above or below the normal river or sea level (assumed range).",
    info_b_desague: "Storm-drain capacity on paved ground: by default, the 1-hour rain exceeded once every 2 years here, measured with the same data (drains are designed for 2 to 10 years; no public network data). 0 = blocked drains.",
